    "html": "<section class=\"context\"><p>At a distance r from the centre of a planet, the gravitational potential due to the planet is V<sub>g</sub> and the gravitational field strength is g.</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"a\"><p><strong>a</strong> Show that <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"V_{g}\"></span> = −g<span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"r\"></span>. [1]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"b\"><p><strong>b</strong> Calculate the magnitude of</p></section><section class=\"context\"><p>For the Earth, the variation of V<sub>g</sub> with r is shown.</p><p><figure data-asset-id=\"fig_01\"><img alt=\"Source markscheme diagram 1\" src=\"asset:fig_01\"/></figure></p><p>A satellite of mass 750 kg orbits the Earth in a circular orbit of radius 1.0 × 10<sup>7</sup> m.</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"b.i\"><p><strong>b.i</strong> the gravitational field strength at the orbital radius of the satellite. [2]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"b.ii\"><p><strong>b.ii</strong> the gravitational force acting on the satellite. [1]</p></section><section class=\"context\"><p>The satellite is moved to a new circular orbit. The orbital radius of the satellite increases by 2.0 km.</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"c.i\"><p><strong>c.i</strong> Outline why the magnitude of the gravitational field strength at the new orbit is approximately the same as at the old orbit. [2]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"c.ii\"><p><strong>c.ii</strong> Determine the change in the total orbital energy of the satellite. [3]</p></section><p><figure data-asset-id=\"fig_01\"><img alt=\"Source question diagram 1\" src=\"asset:fig_01\"/></figure></p>"
